  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>At the far end of the Base Nature François Léotard, Plage du Pacha is one of Fréjus's two official dog beaches (alongside the dog area of <a href="/en/fiche-frejus-argens">Saint-Aygulf/Esclamandes beach</a>, at the other end of the Base Nature but separated by the Argens river mouth, so not reachable on foot from here). It's a peaceful oasis, noticeably quieter than Fréjus's main beaches, with a view of Saint-Aygulf and its surrounding beaches across the water.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Access is on foot only, about 900 metres from the Base Nature car park, via a seafront promenade. The path narrows past the Fréjus military base, which can make it difficult with a stroller. According to the official page of the Fréjus tourist office, the beach is open every day, year-round; dogs are welcome on a leash, with no time restriction mentioned in the official sources consulted. During the summer season (municipal order no. 2026-1797, 4 June to 20 September 2026), article 10 bans dogs from the beaches except on signposted portions, including a zone of the Base Nature beach between channel "I" and lifeguard post no. 6 "Pacha", provided dogs are on a leash. Stay inside that signposted zone. Outside the season the order does not apply and no other published rule was found. From this beach you can watch kitesurfers in the distance on a neighbouring beach.</p>
      <p>The Var departmental tourism board (visitvar.fr) states that Plage du Pacha is reserved for dogs, between the Base Nature and the kite zone, dogs on a leash, swimming allowed; it warns not to go past the kite zone, as the naturist area is just beyond. Order 2026-1797 provides for a "Pacha" lifeguard post in summer, supervised from 1 July to 31 August 2026, 9am to 7pm: while supervision is on, stay within the perimeter marked by the start and end signs. The tourist office page gives 04 94 51 83 83 as contact and a GPS point at the end of the promenade (43.4118 N, 6.7390 E). Nearby, the Villepein ponds can be visited on a leash, but swimming is banned there according to visitvar.fr.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: bring water and shade for you and your dog — this is a secluded beach, reachable only on foot.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The Base Nature François Léotard itself, with its trails and outdoor activities, rounds out the outing nicely before heading back to the car park.</p>
